const advancedLessons=[
 ['computer',1,'PowerShell object anatomy','A command returns objects with named properties. A pipeline transfers those objects; filtering removes rows, projection chooses properties, sorting changes their order, and grouping aggregates. Get-Member discovers object structure; formatting should be left to the final presentation step.','The AZ-040 answer keys progress from command discovery through selection, filtering, sorting and enumeration. In the workbench, compare Id, Department and AccountEnabled before and after each pipeline stage.','course/lab/query'],
 ['ad',3,'AD administrative operations','The AZ-040 and AZ-800 answer keys cover OU creation, groups, users, computer accounts, object moves, domain controller deployment and GPO verification. DistinguishedName identifies a directory location; ObjectGUID identifies an object; SID identifies a security principal. Moving an object changes its DN, not automatically its security entitlement.','Use fixture Get-ADUser -Filter * and filter by Department. Inspect DistinguishedName and Enabled. Explain why Get-ADUser is an AD DS command while Get-MgUser queries Entra. Do not run New-ADUser or other source write commands against real AD.','course/lab/query'],
 ['network',0,'Infrastructure deployment and evidence','The AZ-800 scripts and ARM templates define dependencies and parameters for WAC, VNet routing, disks, DFS and File Sync. A deployment template is a desired control-plane resource graph; executing it may create billed resources. A route determines the next hop, while an NSG evaluates traffic rules.','Use read-only fixture Get-NetIPConfiguration and Get-CimInstance Win32_NetworkAdapterConfiguration. Determine gateway, DNS, DHCPEnabled and IP address; predict failure if public DNS replaces the AD resolver. No scripts are run.','course/lab/query'],
 ['ops',1,'Working with variables, CIM and command pipelines','Variables hold objects; arrays hold ordered values; a hashtable maps keys to values. CIM exposes structured management classes. PowerShell jobs have independent state and output; remote commands execute on another host under an authorized context. A signed script is not automatically safe.','Practice composing supported read-only commands, filtering and grouping the fixture. The AZ-040 source answer keys include actual change, remoting, jobs and licensing commands; here they are reference only. Modern Graph replaces retired AzureAD/MSOnline directory cmdlets; use current help for exact permissions.','course/lab/query'],
 ['ops',0,'Security analytics with KQL','where filters events, project selects fields, sort orders results, summarize count() aggregates rows, distinct returns unique values. A query must use the actual schema, time range and ingestion scope. A detection is an observation, not proof of compromise.','Compose a query for failed sign-ins, then aggregate by user or application. Inspect the returned event records and compare totals. The workbench rejects unsupported operators rather than executing external code.','course/lab/query'],
 ['auth',3,'MSAL from configuration to token acquisition','The sample separates authConfig, popup or redirect sign-in, active-account selection and API token acquisition. clientId identifies an app; authority identifies tenant/identity endpoint; redirectUri is registered callback. A public SPA uses authorization code with PKCE and has no confidential secret. An ID token is for the client, not Graph.','Use the local API workbench to submit an ID token, then an access token intended for Graph. Change audience, expiry, signature status and delegated scope separately; inspect the failing validation boundary.','course/lab/api-inspector'],
 ['apps',0,'Resource-side permission middleware','The sample protected API has authentication middleware, permission helpers, routes and controller. Authentication validates an access token; authorization checks required delegated scope or application role and resource ownership where applicable. A browser-side check or CORS is not an authorization boundary.','Submit a valid modeled token with Payroll.Read, then request Payroll.Export. Compare app-only roles against delegated scopes; show 401 for invalid authentication and 403 for insufficient permission. No real JWTs or network requests.','course/lab/api-inspector'],
 ['govern',2,'HR source and joiner/mover/leaver workflow','The employee lifecycle deck covers HR-driven provisioning, matching and mapping, API-driven inbound provisioning, SaaS provisioning and lifecycle workflows. An employee ID is a stable matching key; hire/leave attributes trigger scheduled tasks; source authority and target account state are distinct. A task can fail even after the source record exists.','In the governance workshop, import a fictional hire, run joiner, change department, rerun mover, then terminate. Check SCIM target state, remove old access and detect alternate grants.','course/lab/iga'],
 ['govern',0,'Employee entitlement and birthright access','The employee access deck adds automatic assignment policies, request approval, custom extensions and recurring access reviews. An automatic assignment rule evaluates attributes; a package assignment can deliver resource roles, while direct access is a separate grant. A custom extension calls an approved external workflow at a supported stage.','Compare automatic IT group assignment with requested Project access; change department and observe automatic removal; test pending approval, denied request, extension failure and retries. No Logic Apps created.','course/lab/iga'],
 ['govern',0,'External identity governance','The partner deck distinguishes self-service sign-up from entitlement requests, connected organizations, sponsor approval, attribute questions, automatic assignment, governance of existing guests and multi-stage recertification. Verification of identity evidence is separate from approval.','Create a modeled contractor request, approve it, test expiry and direct alternate membership. Review should apply a denial to the intended grant, not automatically erase unrelated access.','course/lab/iga'],
 ['govern',1,'Privileged governance and Conditional Access','The privileged identity deck spans directory roles, resource RBAC, group eligibility, activation, review and CA authentication context. Just-in-time controls time; just-enough controls scope. Activation requires policy proof/approval and expiry; alternate grants remain independently effective.','Request a one-hour eligible role. Attempt activation before proof and approval, then activate under policy and advance time. Verify the operation before, during and after active interval.','course/lab/iga'],
 ['apps',1,'Agent provisioning and permission approvals','The agent identity sample separates blueprint, identity request, permission request, approvals, mock provisioning and credentials. A blueprint is a definition, an instance is an identity, a grant is a separate authorization. Provisioning can fail after approval; credentials must remain protected.','In the agent workshop, approve blueprint and identity independently, request a narrow permission, reject an unnecessary write, simulate a provisioning fault and retry, then revoke. No Graph access or development authentication bypass enabled.','course/lab/agent']
];
